(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-pigmented-skin-lesions-vignettes"]={"title":"Pigmented Skin Lesions Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"An 11-month-old with a large congenital naevus over the head and neck develops seizures and vomiting. Which is the most appropriate interpretation?","choices":["Neurocutaneous melanosis has developed, and the prognosis is poor once neurological symptoms appear","Melanoma has arisen within the naevus and has metastasised to the central nervous system","The naevus is unrelated to the symptoms, which need investigating separately","A familial cancer syndrome is present, as suggested by the multiple lesions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — symptoms usually appear within the first few years of life. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman with several dysplastic naevi asks where they are most likely to appear. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["They are common on sun-exposed skin surfaces","They are common on the palms, soles and mucosae","They are common on the dorsal hands, scalp and sacrum","They are common on the face and neck in darker skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they are at least 5 mm with irregular indistinct borders and variable pigmentation. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 46-year-old African American woman has dozens of smooth firm dark brown papules 1 to 3 mm across over both cheeks and the neck, present for years. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dysplastic naevi of the face and neck","Acquired lentigines of the face and neck","Dermatosis papulosa nigrans","Multiple blue naevi of the face"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — identical to small seborrheic keratoses, commonest in this group, with females affected more than males. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman asks how a mole she was born with differs from the ones that appeared later. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["Congenital naevi arise from neural-crest derived precursors migrating along neurovascular bundles; acquired naevi arise from junctional melanocytes","Congenital naevi arise from junctional melanocytes in the epidermis; acquired naevi arise from neural-crest derived precursor cells","Congenital naevi arise from keratinocytes in the basal layer; acquired naevi arise from dermal fibroblasts within the skin","Congenital naevi arise from hair follicle precursors; acquired naevi arise from vascular endothelium in the dermis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — dysplastic naevi are the group with atypical architectural and cytologic features. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man who has worked outdoors for decades has light to dark brown macules with irregular borders on the shoulders, some coalescing where he recalls a severe sunburn. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Solar lentigines","Lentigo simplex","Ephelides","Dermatosis papulosa nigrans"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — they coalesce at sites of severe sunburn and 90% of people have them by fifty. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A newborn has a flat brown patch 14 cm across over the lower back, with a slightly uneven border and a pebbly surface. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A naevus spilus of the lower back","A café-au-lait macule of the trunk","A dysplastic melanocytic naevus","A large congenital melanocytic naevus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — evident at birth or shortly after, and the larger the lesion the higher the melanoma risk. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with several solar lentigines wants them removed. Which options are appropriate?","choices":["Narrow band ultraviolet B phototherapy three times weekly","Retinoids, cryotherapy or quality-switched laser","Topical fluorouracil, imiquimod or tirbanibulin","Excision with clear margins under local anaesthetic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — treatment is not necessary, and removal is for cosmetic reasons. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with vitiligo has been using a topical corticosteroid on her eyelids for several months. Which risks should be checked for?","choices":["Increased skin cancer risk and photosensitivity","Teratogenicity and the need for pregnancy testing","Tooth staining and hepatotoxicity from the agent","Skin atrophy and raised intraocular pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a topical calcineurin inhibitor suits areas where steroids cannot be applied. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has about forty moles, most 3 to 5 mm, evenly coloured, round and sharply demarcated. He asks whether he has too many. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Numbers this high define dysplastic naevus syndrome, which requires over one hundred by adolescence","Numbers peak in the thirties and then decline, but melanoma risk rises with the number, so sun protection and monitoring matter","Numbers continue to rise throughout life, and any patient with over twenty naevi needs them all excised early","Numbers are unrelated to melanoma risk, so no monitoring or sun protection is required at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — light skin tone and a tendency to sunburn increase the number. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"The parents of a child with naevus spilus ask about follow-up. Which is the most appropriate plan?","choices":["Excision of the whole patch before adolescence to eliminate the melanoma risk","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain and total spine at the first visit","Biopsy of each darker macule within the patch to exclude atypical features","Observation with periodic clinical evaluation, plus sun protection counselling"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lesion rarely progresses to melanoma. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 3-year-old has a small congenital melanocytic naevus on the forearm with no neurological symptoms. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Cryotherapy of the lesion, since it removes pigmented lesions without leaving a scar","Observation, since management balances melanoma risk with cosmetic and functional considerations","Immediate excision, since every congenital naevus carries an equally high melanoma risk","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain, since all congenital naevi warrant it at diagnosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — smaller lesions carry lower melanoma risk than larger ones. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A newborn has a large pigmented patch across the posterior midline of the neck and upper back. What does the deck require next?","choices":["Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain, with or without total spine, matched to where the naevus sits","Immediate excision of the whole lesion before the child reaches six months of age or walks","Observation alone, with review when the child starts school","Skin biopsy of the darkest area to exclude melanoma at birth"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — head, neck or posterior midline lesions raise neurocutaneous melanosis. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with newly diagnosed vitiligo is having her workup arranged. Which investigations are appropriate?","choices":["Punch biopsy of the leading edge sent for direct immunofluorescence and antigen mapping","Minimal erythema dose testing to ultraviolet A and to ultraviolet B before treatment","Potassium hydroxide preparation of a skin scraping taken from the affected patches","Wood's lamp examination in a dark room, with a complete blood count and antinuclear antibody"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the labs address the autoimmune diseases associated with vitiligo. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has vitiligo involving about 3% of her body surface, mostly on the face and neck. Which topical option best suits those sites?","choices":["A topical depigmenting agent such as hydroquinone or an alpha-hydroxy acid","A topical retinoid applied nightly to the affected areas of skin","A topical calcineurin inhibitor such as tacrolimus or pimecrolimus","A high-potency topical corticosteroid applied twice daily to the face"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they suit areas where steroids cannot be applied, though they carry an increased cancer risk. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has a 2 cm blue-grey nodule on the sacral region suspected to be a cellular blue naevus. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Clinical diagnosis and observation, since blue naevi are always benign lesions","Cryotherapy for cosmetic removal, since the lesion sits on a visible area","Magnetic resonance imaging of the spine, given the sacral location of it","Biopsy, since diagnosis is clinical for small lesions but by biopsy for larger ones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — observation follows, with biopsy or excision if changes are noted. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has vitiligo affecting about fifteen per cent of his body surface. He has used topical steroids without benefit. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Narrow-band ultraviolet B phototherapy, ideally combined with topical therapy","Psoralen plus ultraviolet A, since it is more effective than narrow-band ultraviolet B","Tissue grafting to the affected areas","Continue the same topical steroid for a further six months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — phototherapy is first line above five per cent, and combination therapy is described as ideal. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man wants several waxy stuck-on lesions on his trunk removed because they catch on his clothing. Which options does the deck give?","choices":["Topical imiquimod five nights weekly for six weeks","No intervention is available for symptomatic lesions","Retinoids, cryotherapy or quality-switched laser, since removal here is elective","Excision with a two-millimetre margin, because they are premalignant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — treatment is not necessary; removal is for cosmetic or symptomatic reasons. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman with dermatosis papulosa nigrans asks about removing the lesions. Which is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Wide excision is needed because these lesions can transform into melanoma over time","They are best left untreated; excision, curettage or laser can be used, but cryotherapy is avoided","Cryotherapy at the first visit is the treatment of choice, with laser reserved for recurrences","Topical depigmenting agents should be tried first, since the lesions are pigmented macules"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — cryotherapy risks post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation in this group. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 39-year-old Polynesian woman with facial papules asks why her doctor will not freeze them off as a friend had done elsewhere. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["Cryotherapy is avoided in this condition because of post-inflammatory hyperpigmentation","Cryotherapy is avoided because the lesions are too small for it to be practical at all","Cryotherapy is avoided because it would risk transforming the lesions into melanoma","Cryotherapy is avoided because it would spread the lesions across the rest of the face"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — excision, curettage or laser can be used where treatment is wanted. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has generalised darkly pigmented lentigines over much of his body. Which additional consideration does this raise?","choices":["An internal malignancy should be considered, and urgent screening arranged","An inherited disorder should be considered, as with LAMB and myxoma syndrome","Neurocutaneous melanosis should be considered, and brain imaging arranged","Dysplastic naevus syndrome should be considered, and every lesion biopsied"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a partial or generalised lentigo is what prompts that question. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man's family are alarmed by dozens of dark warty growths that appeared on his back over two years. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["They are a sign of an underlying internal malignancy and warrant urgent systemic investigation","They are contagious growths spread by contact and will need isolation until they are treated","They are benign, common in older adults, and the diagnosis is clinical without any need for biopsy","They are precancerous and each one carries a measurable chance of becoming a squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — cryotherapy helps if a lesion becomes itchy or inflamed, though it may recur. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man with vitiligo says he has stopped going out because of how his skin looks, and that his doctor called it purely cosmetic. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["The condition will resolve spontaneously within a year, so no treatment is needed","The psychological and social impact is real, and psychological intervention is part of management","The condition is indeed cosmetic, so treatment is optional and reassurance is enough","The condition indicates an underlying malignancy, which explains how unwell he feels"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — low self-esteem and poor body image are named explicitly. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 10-year-old girl has a lesion clinically suggestive of Spitz naevus. Why can it not simply be observed?","choices":["It always transforms into melanoma if it is left in place beyond adolescence","It grows continuously and will reach several centimetres if left untreated","It is contagious and can spread to other family members through contact","It sometimes resembles melanoma, so diagnosis is by biopsy or wide excision"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — management is then by excision. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman is about to have an irritated seborrhoeic keratosis frozen. What should she be told beforehand, and why does it matter?","choices":["That the site will remain permanently depigmented","That she will need a confirmatory biopsy afterwards to establish the diagnosis","That the lesion can come back after freezing, so she is not surprised or disappointed later","That freezing is curative and the lesion will not return"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — she framed this as protecting the relationship as much as informing the patient. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with numerous dysplastic naevi asks what he should do between clinic visits. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Use sun protection and report any lesion that changes or is newly developing","Have every naevus excised over the coming year to eliminate the melanoma risk","Avoid all sun exposure entirely, since photoprotection alone will clear the naevi","Apply a topical depigmenting agent to the naevi to lighten their appearance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — management is observation, biopsy of changing lesions and excision where melanoma is a concern. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man with vitiligo asks whether PUVA would work better than the narrow band ultraviolet B he has been offered. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["PUVA is preferred, because it produces faster repigmentation across a much larger body surface area","Neither is used in vitiligo, since phototherapy risks worsening the depigmented patches","Both are equivalent, so the choice can be made purely on cost and on availability","Narrow band ultraviolet B is preferred, because PUVA carries adverse effects including increased skin cancer risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — combining phototherapy with topical therapy is described as ideal. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has a biopsy-confirmed pigmented spindle cell naevus of the thigh. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Cryotherapy for cosmetic improvement","Sun protection counselling alone","Excision with negative margins","Observation with annual photographs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lesion is benign but excision with clear margins is the described management. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman wants a mole on her back removed because her bra strap catches it. Which is the most appropriate response?","choices":["Removal must be preceded by a biopsy to confirm the lesion is not a melanoma","Removal is mandatory for any naevus subject to repeated friction from clothing","Removal for symptomatic or cosmetic reasons is reasonable, alongside sun protection counselling","Removal is not offered for symptoms, since a benign naevus should always be left alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is clinical and management is otherwise observation. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A newborn has a large congenital melanocytic naevus over the posterior midline of the neck and scalp. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Skin biopsy of the lesion to confirm that it is melanocytic in its origin","Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain, with or without total spine, matched to the location of the naevus","Excision of the lesion in the neonatal period before any imaging is arranged","Observation with clinical photographs, repeating the whole assessment at twelve months of age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the concern is neurocutaneous melanosis. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man with freckles asks whether laser treatment will get rid of them permanently. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["No treatment exists, so the lesions must simply be accepted as they are","Laser and intense pulsed light are preferred over cryotherapy, but the lesions can relapse","Laser removes the lesions permanently, so no further sun protection will be needed","Cryotherapy is the preferred option because it treats each lesion individually"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — sun protection and education remain the foundation of management. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 7"}]};
